WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.600
Next in Unit 1 is the decision path.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.001
Answer questions one to eight in
order; the first yes is the target,

3
00:00:07.001 --> 00:00:10.587
and each target lines up with
a Chapter 3 disposition.

4
00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:15.013
An account that's disabled, unused
or without an owner is retired.

5
00:00:15.013 --> 00:00:17.929
A workload on Azure gets
a managed identity;

6
00:00:17.929 --> 00:00:20.603
one in CI/CD gets a federated credential;

7
00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:25.952
one on premises that calls Graph gets the
Arc identity or a certificate, and if it

8
00:00:25.952 --> 00:00:31.462
also needs Kerberos, it goes on to question
five as well and takes two dispositions.

9
00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:33.024
Next come the exceptions.

10
00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:36.264
An account that's already a
G M S A or D M S A is kept,

11
00:00:36.264 --> 00:00:39.417
and one that must stay
a traditional account,

12
00:00:39.417 --> 00:00:43.764
because the service supports no
managed account or relies on

13
00:00:43.764 --> 00:00:48.537
unconstrained delegation, is kept
on Kerberos; each with an end date.

14
00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:52.481
Then a traditional account
that needs Kerberos,

15
00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:58.888
with a Windows Server 2025 domain controller
and every host on 2025, gets a D M S A.

16
00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:04.411
Any other case that needs Kerberos, a
standalone account included, gets a G M S A.

17
00:01:04.411 --> 00:01:08.467
Question nine applies to every
target from five to eight:

18
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:14.076
Kerberos to a resource with no Entra
equivalent also puts it on the island list.

19
00:01:14.076 --> 00:01:17.943
Then the track's rules, which
a hand migration follows too:

20
00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:22.059
two named owners and a sponsor;
a reviewed disposition first;

21
00:01:22.059 --> 00:01:27.326
a thirty-day parallel run, then disable,
and never delete during the migration;

22
00:01:27.326 --> 00:01:32.160
no client secrets; no privileged
groups; and changes that are reviewed.
